/**
 * @file files-service.js
 * @brief The filesystem domain layer of the CloudAvid File Portal: resolves FILES_ROOT, sanitises every client-supplied path, walks the download tree, and turns the real disk into the data behind all of server.js's JSON and download APIs.
 *
 * @tableofcontents
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @section fs_overview 📖 Overview
 *
 * @subsection fs_eli5 🧒 ELI5 Summary
 *
 * Imagine the portal is a **library**, and `server.js` is the librarian at
 * the front desk. Where do the books actually live? In the basement. This
 * file is the **basement keeper**: the only person with the key to the
 * storage room.
 *
 * The librarian never walks into the basement. When a visitor asks "what
 * shelves do you have?" the librarian shouts downstairs, and the basement
 * keeper walks the aisles, counts the books, checks the newest arrival
 * dates, and sends a neat list back up. When a visitor asks "hand me that
 * exact box", the basement keeper first checks the request slip very
 * carefully — nobody is allowed to ask for the box marked `..` ("two rooms
 * back"), and nobody may leave the storage room. If anything is wrong — the
 * basement is locked (`FILES_ROOT` missing), it is empty, or the slip is
 * invalid — the keeper sends back a stamped slip of paper saying exactly why
 * (`403 PATH_TRAVERSAL`, `503 FILES_ROOT_EMPTY`, …), which the librarian
 * forwards to the visitor.
 *
 * @subsection fs_responsibilities Core Responsibilities
 *
 * - **Sole Owner of FILES_ROOT:** Reads `process.env.FILES_ROOT` (populated
 *   from `.env` by `lib/load-env.js`), resolves it against the project root,
 *   and validates that it exists, is a directory, and is non-empty with at
 *   least one visible category folder. No other file in the repository
 *   resolves or stats `FILES_ROOT` except via this module.
 * - **Path Safety Wall:** Converts any client-supplied relative path into a
 *   validated absolute path that is provably inside `FILES_ROOT`, rejecting
 *   `.` / `..` segments and NUL bytes, and re-checking with
 *   `path.relative()` after resolution (defence in depth).
 * - **Directory Listing Engine:** `listCategories()` (with per-category
 *   recursive summaries) and `listPath()` (flat listings at any depth) —
 *   the data behind `/api/categories` and `/api/files`.
 * - **Download Resolver:** `getDownloadTarget()` stats a file and returns
 *   the `{ absPath, fileName, size, mtime }` bundle that `server.js`
 *   streams with `fs.createReadStream`. This module never streams; it only
 *   decides *what* may be streamed.
 * - **Search Engine:** `searchEntries()` — an iterative, depth-capped
 *   DFS over the tree with substring name matching, hard entry/limit caps
 *   and a `truncated` flag — the data behind `/api/search`.
 * - **Release Feed:** `listLatestReleases()` — a full-tree walk that ranks
 *   every visible file by modification time — the data behind `/api/releases`.
 * - **Synchronous Health Probe:** `peekRootSync()` — a never-throwing,
 *   synchronous snapshot of FILES_ROOT health for `/api/health` and the
 *   startup banner.
 * - **Structured Failure Contract:** `serviceError()` stamps every failure
 *   with an `httpStatus` and a stable machine-readable `code`, which
 *   `server.js`'s `sendServiceError()` forwards verbatim as JSON.
 * - **Hidden-Entry Filter:** `isHidden()` enforces the portal-wide rule
 *   that `index.html`, `index.php` and dotfiles never appear in any listing
 *   anywhere in the product.
 *
 * @subsection fs_arch_role 🧭 Role in the Overall Architecture
 *
 * This file is the **bottom of the back-end** and the **only file that
 * touches the downloads tree**. The deliberate two-layer split of this
 * project is:
 *
 * - `server.js` = transport layer (HTTP parsing, headers, CORS, streaming,
 *   routing, logging). Knows nothing about how paths are validated.
 * - `lib/files-service.js` = domain layer (this file). Knows nothing about
 *   HTTP; it never writes headers and never sees a request object. Its only
 *   HTTP awareness is the `httpStatus` numbers it stamps onto errors, a
 *   pragmatic exception that lets `server.js`'s generic `catch` render the
 *   right status without a mapping table.
 *
 * Upward, it is consumed exclusively by `server.js`, which wires each
 * exported function to exactly one API route. Downward, it depends only on
 * Node built-ins (`fs/promises`, `fs`, `path`) and on the environment
 * contract established by `lib/load-env.js`. The frontend never calls this
 * module; it consumes its output indirectly through
 * `assets/js/services/resource-service.js`.
 *
 * @note What This File Does NOT Do:
 * - It does **not** serve HTTP. No routing, no headers, no streaming — all
 *   of that is `server.js` (`streamDownload`, `sendJSON`).
 * - It does **not** cache. Every call re-reads the disk; all caching in this
 *   product lives in the browser (`resource-service.js`'s sessionStorage).
 * - It does **not** enforce the browser-side `isHidden()` copy — that is a
 *   duplicated display-level filter in `assets/js/browse.js` and
 *   `assets/js/services/resource-service.js`. This module's filter is the
 *   authoritative one; the frontend ones are cosmetic backups.
 * - It does **not** read or write file contents. It only stats, lists and
 *   resolves paths; even `getDownloadTarget()` returns metadata, and the
 *   bytes are streamed by `server.js`.
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @section fs_arch 🏗️ Architectural Flow
 *
 * The diagram below shows how one service call flows: every public function
 * funnels through the same three gates — root resolution, structure
 * validation (where applicable), and the path-safety wall — before any
 * disk read happens.
 *
 * @dot
 * digraph service_flow {
 *   rankdir=TB;
 *   node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, color="#9CA3AF"];
 *   edge [fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=9, color="#6B7280"];
 *
 *   caller [label="server.js API handlers\\n(6 routes)", fillcolor="#DBEAFE"];
 *
 *   subgraph cluster_gates {
 *     label="Shared validation gates (every call)";
 *     style="dashed"; color="#9CA3AF"; fontname="Helvetica"; fontsize=11;
 *
 *     envcheck [label="resolveConfiguredRoot()\\nFILES_ROOT missing → 503\\nFILES_ROOT_MISSING", fillcolor="#FEF3C7"];
 *     rootstat [label="getRoot()\\nstat + isDirectory\\n503 FILES_ROOT_NOT_FOUND /\\nFILES_ROOT_NOT_DIRECTORY", fillcolor="#FEF3C7"];
 *     struct   [label="assertCategoryStructure()\\n503 FILES_ROOT_EMPTY /\\nFILES_ROOT_NO_CATEGORIES", fillcolor="#FEF3C7"];
 *     pathwall [label="resolveInsideRoot()\\n. · .. · NUL → 400 INVALID_PATH\\noutside root → 403 PATH_TRAVERSAL", fillcolor="#FEF3C7"];
 *   }
 *
 *   subgraph cluster_ops {
 *     label="Domain operations";
 *     style="dashed"; color="#9CA3AF"; fontname="Helvetica"; fontsize=11;
 *
 *     cats    [label="listCategories()\\n+ summarizeTree() per category", fillcolor="#CFFAFE"];
 *     listing [label="listPath()\\nreadVisibleEntries + fs.stat per entry", fillcolor="#CFFAFE"];
 *     dl      [label="getDownloadTarget()\\nstat → {absPath,fileName,size,mtime}", fillcolor="#CFFAFE"];
 *     search  [label="searchEntries()\\niterative DFS · nameMatches\\nSEARCH_LIMIT · MAX_WALK_ENTRIES", fillcolor="#CFFAFE"];
 *     rel     [label="listLatestReleases()\\nrecursive walk · sort by mtime", fillcolor="#CFFAFE"];
 *     peek    [label="peekRootSync()\\nfsSync · never throws", fillcolor="#CFFAFE"];
 *   }
 *
 *   disk [label="Real filesystem\\n(FILES_ROOT tree)", shape=cylinder, fillcolor="#FFEDD5"];
 *   out  [label="Plain data structures /\\nserviceError-stamped rejections", fillcolor="#D1FAE5"];
 *   back [label="server.js\\nsendJSON · streamDownload · sendServiceError", fillcolor="#D1FAE5"];
 *
 *   caller -> cats;
 *   caller -> listing;
 *   caller -> dl;
 *   caller -> search;
 *   caller -> rel;
 *   caller -> peek [style=dashed, label="sync, no gates"];
 *
 *   cats -> envcheck;
 *   listing -> envcheck;
 *   dl -> envcheck;
 *   search -> envcheck;
 *   rel -> envcheck;
 *   envcheck -> rootstat;
 *   cats -> struct;
 *   search -> struct;
 *   rel -> struct;
 *   listing -> pathwall;
 *   dl -> pathwall;
 *   rootstat -> disk [style=dashed, label="validated root"];
 *   struct -> disk;
 *   pathwall -> disk;
 *   search -> disk;
 *   rel -> disk;
 *   cats -> out;
 *   listing -> out;
 *   dl -> out;
 *   search -> out;
 *   rel -> out;
 *   peek -> out;
 *   out -> back;
 * }
 * @enddot
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @section fs_sequence 🔄 Execution Sequence
 *
 * The sequence below traces the two most important runtime conversations:
 * a browse request (category → folder → download) and a search, showing how
 * `server.js` interleaves this module with its own transport helpers and
 * how the browser arrives here via the frontend service layer.
 *
 * @startuml
 * skinparam backgroundColor transparent
 * skinparam sequence {
 *   ArrowColor #6B7280
 *   LifeLineBorderColor #9CA3AF
 *   ParticipantBackgroundColor #DBEAFE
 *   ParticipantBorderColor #9CA3AF
 *   ActorBackgroundColor #E0E7FF
 *   ActorBorderColor #9CA3AF
 * }
 *
 * actor "Visitor's Browser\n(home.js · browse.js)" as Browser
 * participant "assets/js/services/\nresource-service.js" as ResSvc
 * participant "server.js\n(HTTP transport)" as Server
 * participant "lib/files-service.js\n(this file)" as FSvc
 * participant "FILES_ROOT\nreal filesystem" as Disk
 *
 * == Browse flow ==
 * Browser -> ResSvc : getResources()
 * ResSvc -> Server : GET /api/categories
 * Server -> FSvc : listCategories(PROJECT_ROOT)
 * FSvc -> FSvc : getRoot() → resolveConfiguredRoot()\n+ stat + isDirectory
 * FSvc -> Disk : readdir(FILES_ROOT, withFileTypes)
 * FSvc -> FSvc : assertCategoryStructure()
 * loop for each visible category folder
 *   FSvc -> Disk : summarizeTree() — readdir + stat walk\n(depth ≤ MAX_WALK_DEPTH)
 * end
 * FSvc --> Server : sorted category array (name, fileCount, folderCount,\nlatestModified)
 * Server --> ResSvc : 200 JSON (CORS + no-store)
 * ResSvc --> Browser : mapped resource cards\n(sessionStorage cache, TTL 5 min)
 *
 * Browser -> ResSvc : listFiles(category)
 * ResSvc -> Server : GET /api/files/<category>
 * Server -> FSvc : listPath(PROJECT_ROOT, rel)
 * FSvc -> FSvc : resolveInsideRoot() — reject . · .. · NUL,\nre-check with path.relative()
 * FSvc -> Disk : readdir(target) + stat per visible entry
 * FSvc --> Server : flat entry array (name, path, type, size, mtime)
 * Server --> ResSvc : 200 JSON
 * ResSvc --> Browser : rendered list rows
 *
 * Browser -> ResSvc : downloadHref(rel)
 * ResSvc -> Server : GET /api/download/<category>/<file>
 * Server -> FSvc : getDownloadTarget(PROJECT_ROOT, rel)
 * FSvc -> FSvc : resolveInsideRoot() again
 * FSvc -> Disk : stat(target)
 * FSvc --> Server : { absPath, fileName, size, mtime }
 * Server -> Disk : fs.createReadStream(absPath)
 * Server --> Browser : binary stream + Content-Disposition
 *
 * == Search flow ==
 * Browser -> ResSvc : search(q)
 * ResSvc -> Server : GET /api/search?q=…&limit=…
 * Server -> FSvc : searchEntries(PROJECT_ROOT, q, { limit })
 * FSvc -> Disk : iterative DFS — readdir per visited dir\n(≤ MAX_WALK_ENTRIES entries, depth ≤ MAX_WALK_DEPTH)
 * FSvc --> Server : { results, scanned, truncated }
 * Server --> ResSvc : 200 JSON
 * ResSvc --> Browser : suggestion dropdown (autocomplete.js)\nor search results (home.js)
 * @enduml
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @section fs_blocks 🧩 Block-by-Block Deep Dive
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @subsection fs_sub_requires 🧩 Block: Core Module Requires (fs/promises, fs, path)
 *
 * @par 🎯 Purpose
 * Load the three Node built-ins this module is built from: the promise API
 * for all async directory work, the callback/sync API for the one
 * synchronous probe, and the path toolkit for every safety check.
 *
 * @par 🧒 ELI5
 * The basement keeper's three tools: a **walkie-talkie that texts back
 * later** (`fs/promises` — "I'll tell you when the shelf count is done"),
 * an **instant-answer phone** (`fs` — "right now: does the room exist,
 * yes or no"), and a **map of the building** (`path`) so every route
 * between rooms is drawn the same way on every operating system.
 *
 * @par ⚙️ How It Works (Step-by-Step)
 * 1. `fs/promises` is required and bound to the name `fs`: every listing,
 *    stat and walk in this file is `async` and uses the promise API, which
 *    keeps the hot paths (concurrent `Promise.all` listings) composable.
 * 2. The classic callback module is required **separately** as `fsSync` and
 *    used in exactly one place: `peekRootSync()`, which must run inside a
 *    synchronous request handler and inside the startup banner without
 *    async/await plumbing.
 * 3. `path` is required once and powers every join, resolve, relative and
 *    separator handling decision — critical because `FILES_ROOT` may be a
 *    Windows path (`D:\CloudAvid\Downloads` per `.env.example`) while all
 *    API-facing paths are POSIX-style.
 *
 * @par 📥 Inputs
 * - None (module loading only).
 *
 * @par 📤 Outputs
 * - Three module-local bindings used by every other block in this file.
 *
 * @par 🔗 Project Dependencies
 * - Depends on: Node.js runtime only — no npm dependency, which is why the
 *   domain layer keeps working even if `node_modules` is wiped.
 * - Consumed by: nothing at require time; the bindings are used by all
 *   functions documented below.
 * - Sideways: mirrors `server.js`'s "built-ins first" require style and
 *   `lib/load-env.js`'s identical approach.
 * - @see server.js
 * - @see lib/load-env.js
 *
 * @par ⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
 * If `fsSync` is removed (e.g. during an async-only refactor),
 * `peekRootSync()` throws a `ReferenceError` the first time `/api/health`
 * is hit or the startup banner renders — health checks start reporting
 * `degraded` and monitoring silently flips. Conversely, replacing
 * `require("fs/promises")` with `require("fs")` breaks every `await` in
 * this file at runtime: the calls return callbacks-style behaviour and all
 * six API routes start 500-ing.
 *
 * @par 💻 Live Source Code:
 * @snippet lib/files-service.js fs_snip_requires
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @subsection fs_sub_limits 🧩 Block: Operational Limit Constants
 *
 * @par 🎯 Purpose
 * Centralise the guard-rail numbers that keep runaway directories, huge
 * trees and over-broad searches from consuming unbounded time or memory.
 *
 * @par 🧒 ELI5
 * Speed limits posted at the basement door: "you may check at most 8000
 * boxes on one trip, you may descend at most 12 floors, you may put 200
 * search results on the trolley, and 12 items on the new-arrivals shelf."
 * Posted once so every worker follows the same rules.
 *
 * @par ⚙️ How It Works (Step-by-Step)
 * 1. `MAX_WALK_ENTRIES = 8000` caps how many *visible entries* the search
 *    and release walks may visit; both loops stop mid-walk when reached.
 * 2. `MAX_WALK_DEPTH = 12` caps recursion/stack depth in `summarizeTree`,
 *    `searchEntries` and `listLatestReleases`; deeper directories are
 *    silently skipped rather than erroring.
 * 3. `SEARCH_LIMIT = 200` is the default *and* the hard maximum result
 *    count for `searchEntries`; a client-passed `limit` is clamped with
 *    `Math.min` so it can never raise the ceiling.
 * 4. `RELEASES_LIMIT = 12` is the default cap for `listLatestReleases`
 *    (hard maximum 50, enforced separately inside that function).
 *
 * @par 📥 Inputs
 * - None (compile-time constants).
 *
 * @par 📤 Outputs
 * - Bounds consumed by `summarizeTree`, `searchEntries`,
 *   `listLatestReleases`.
 *
 * @par 🔗 Project Dependencies
 * - Depends on: nothing.
 * - Consumed by: the three walking functions below; indirectly by the
 *   frontend (`resource-service.js` caps its own suggestion request at
 *   `limit: 6`, always under the ceiling).
 * - @see assets/js/services/resource-service.js
 *
 * @par ⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
 * These numbers are part of the *observable behaviour* of three API routes.
 * Raising `MAX_WALK_ENTRIES` changes the worst-case latency of
 * `/api/search` and `/api/releases` (a huge tree = slow responses on the
 * request thread); lowering `SEARCH_LIMIT` below the frontend's request
 * limits (e.g. `limit=6` suggestions) silently truncates autocomplete.
 * `MAX_WALK_DEPTH` interacts with the repository layout: deeper than 12
 * nested category folders will *vanish* from search and releases, which
 * looks like a data bug, not a config change.
 *
 * @par 💻 Live Source Code:
 * @snippet lib/files-service.js fs_snip_limits
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @subsection fs_sub_ishidden 🧩 Function: `isHidden(name)`
 *
 * @par 🎯 Purpose
 * Define, in exactly one place, which filenames are invisible to the whole
 * portal: index pages (which would leak as "files" next to real downloads)
 * and every dotfile.
 *
 * @par 🧒 ELI5
 * The keeper's ignore-list taped inside the storage room door: anything
 * called "index" and anything starting with a dot is scenery, not
 * merchandise — even if a visitor asks for it by name, it is not on any
 * shelf list.
 *
 * @par ⚙️ How It Works (Step-by-Step)
 * 1. Coerce the argument with `String(name || "")` so `undefined` or empty
 *    names are treated as hidden rather than crashing a walk.
 * 2. Lower-case once (`l`) so `INDEX.HTML` on a case-insensitive Windows
 *    volume is filtered identically to `index.html`.
 * 3. Hide when: the name is falsy, or is `index.html`, or is
 *    `index.php`, or starts with `.` (catches `.git`, `.env`, `.htaccess`,
 *    and macOS `.DS_Store`).
 * 4. Note the asymmetric test style: `index.html`/`index.php` compare the
 *    lower-cased string, while the dotfile check uses the original `name`
 *    — dotfiles are conventionally lower-case, and `startsWith(".")` is
 *    case-agnostic anyway.
 *
 * @par 📥 Inputs
 * - `name`: a raw directory-entry name (string or falsy).
 *
 * @par 📤 Outputs
 * - `boolean` — `true` means "skip this entry in every listing, walk and
 *   search result".
 *
 * @par 🔗 Project Dependencies
 * - Depends on: nothing.
 * - Consumed by: `readVisibleEntries`, `summarizeTree`'s walk,
 *   `searchEntries`, `listLatestReleases`'s walk — i.e. every disk-reading
 *   path in this file.
 * - Sideways: this is the *authoritative* copy of the rule. The frontend
 *   re-implements it defensively in `resource-service.js` (`isHidden`
 *   used by `listFiles`) and in `browse.js` (filtering API rows again at
 *   render time).
 * - @see assets/js/services/resource-service.js
 * - @see assets/js/browse.js
 *
 * @par ⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
 * Adding a name here hides it from *every* surface at once — categories,
 * listings, search, releases and download *listings* (though not downloads
 * themselves, which go through `getDownloadTarget` and do not consult this
 * function). Removing, say, the `index.html` rule makes web-server index
 * files appear as downloadable products. Note the frontend has its own
 * copies: changing only this function leaves `browse.js` still filtering
 * the old list, so surfaces can disagree after a partial edit.
 *
 * @par 💻 Live Source Code:
 * @snippet lib/files-service.js fs_snip_ishidden
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @subsection fs_sub_serviceerror 🧩 Function: `serviceError(httpStatus, code, message)`
 *
 * @par 🎯 Purpose
 * The module's single error factory: every failure this file raises is an
 * `Error` enriched with an HTTP status and a stable machine-readable code,
 * forming the API's error contract.
 *
 * @par 🧒 ELI5
 * Pre-printed rejection slips. Instead of the keeper shouting "something
 * went wrong!", every refusal gets a stamp: the desk knows how serious it
 * is (the number) and a short code the visitor's software can recognise
 * (like `PATH_TRAVERSAL`).
 *
 * @par ⚙️ How It Works (Step-by-Step)
 * 1. Construct a plain `new Error(message)` — the message becomes the
 *    human-readable `error` field of the JSON response.
 * 2. Attach `err.httpStatus` — the status `server.js`'s catch block uses
 *    as `err.httpStatus || 500`.
 * 3. Attach `err.code` — a SCREAMING_SNAKE identifier that `server.js`
 *    forwards verbatim as the `code` field, letting the frontend (and
 *    humans reading API docs) branch on exact failure kinds.
 * 4. No stack manipulation, no custom class — deliberately lightweight so
 *    ordinary `instanceof Error` checks and log formatting keep working.
 *
 * @par 📥 Inputs
 * - `httpStatus`: number (400/403/404/503 in this file).
 * - `code`: stable identifier — `INVALID_PATH`, `PATH_TRAVERSAL`,
 *   `NOT_FOUND`, `NOT_A_DIRECTORY`, `NOT_A_FILE`, `FILES_ROOT_MISSING`,
 *   `FILES_ROOT_NOT_FOUND`, `FILES_ROOT_NOT_DIRECTORY`, `FILES_ROOT_EMPTY`,
 *   `FILES_ROOT_NO_CATEGORIES`.
 * - `message`: end-user-presentable sentence (surfaced verbatim in JSON).
 *
 * @par 📤 Outputs
 * - An `Error` with `httpStatus` and `code` properties, meant to be thrown.
 *
 * @par 🔗 Project Dependencies
 * - Depends on: nothing.
 * - Consumed by: literally every throwing block in this file; and
 *   indirectly by `server.js`'s `sendServiceError(res, err)`, which reads
 *   exactly these two properties.
 * - @see server.js
 *
 * @par ⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
 * The `code` strings are a public API contract: `resource-service.js`'s
 * `parseError()` surfaces them to users and tests/monitors may match on
 * them. Renaming a code (or changing an `httpStatus`) alters API responses
 * with no compile-time signal. If a new failure path throws a *plain*
 * `Error` instead of using this factory, `server.js` renders it as
 * `500 INTERNAL` — an accurate status but a debugging dead-end.
 *
 * @par 💻 Live Source Code:
 * @snippet lib/files-service.js fs_snip_serviceerror
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @subsection fs_sub_resolveconfig 🧩 Function: `resolveConfiguredRoot(projectRoot)`
 *
 * @par 🎯 Purpose
 * Turn the raw `FILES_ROOT` environment string into an absolute directory
 * path, failing fast with a dedicated error when the setting is absent.
 *
 * @par 🧒 ELI5
 * Reading the label on the basement door. No label? Refuse to guess — go
 * back and tell the manager "the door has no name on it" (`503
 * FILES_ROOT_MISSING`). Label can be a full address or directions from the
 * lobby; the keeper knows both dialects.
 *
 * @par ⚙️ How It Works (Step-by-Step)
 * 1. Read `process.env.FILES_ROOT` — populated at boot by
 *    `lib/load-env.js` before `server.js` requires this module (both are
 *    required after `loadEnv()` runs, so ordering is safe).
 * 2. Treat `undefined`, `null` and whitespace-only values (`""` after
 *    `String(raw).trim()`) as "not configured" → throw
 *    `503 FILES_ROOT_MISSING`. 503 (not 500) is deliberate: it tells the
 *    operator this is a *configuration* problem, and it reads correctly on
 *    `/api/health`-style probes.
 * 3. Trim once into `configured`; whitespace around the value in `.env` is
 *    therefore harmless.
 * 4. If the string is already absolute (`path.isAbsolute` — true for
 *    `/srv/cloudavid/downloads` on Linux and `D:\CloudAvid\Downloads` on
 *    Windows), resolve it with `path.resolve(configured)` to normalise
 *    separators and collapses.
 * 5. Otherwise anchor it to the supplied `projectRoot` (`__dirname` of
 *    `server.js`) with `path.resolve(projectRoot, configured)` — this is
 *    why `.env.example`'s relative default `./sample-downloads` works from
 *    any working directory.
 *
 * @par 📥 Inputs
 * - `projectRoot`: absolute base directory (always `PROJECT_ROOT` from
 *   `server.js`).
 * - Environment: `FILES_ROOT` (documented in `.env.example`).
 *
 * @par 📤 Outputs
 * - Absolute, normalised `root` path string.
 * - Throws `503 FILES_ROOT_MISSING` when unset/blank.
 *
 * @par 🔗 Project Dependencies
 * - Depends on: `process.env.FILES_ROOT` (written by `lib/load-env.js`),
 *   Node `path`, `serviceError`.
 * - Consumed by: `getRoot` (async path) and `peekRootSync` (sync path) —
 *   the only two call sites.
 * - @see lib/load-env.js
 * - @see .env.example
 * - @see server.js
 *
 * @par ⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
 * This is the seam between deployment configuration and the whole product.
 * Renaming the `FILES_ROOT` key breaks every deployment's `.env` at once;
 * removing the trim makes `"D:\Downloads "` (trailing space) resolve to a
 * different directory on Windows; changing the relative-path anchor from
 * `projectRoot` to `process.cwd()` breaks `npm start` from any other
 * working directory and any service manager that starts the app from
 * elsewhere. Security note: it intentionally does *not* validate *what*
 * the path is — pointing `FILES_ROOT` at the source tree would expose the
 * application itself, which is exactly what `.env.example`'s comment warns
 * against.
 *
 * @par 💻 Live Source Code:
 * @snippet lib/files-service.js fs_snip_resolveconfig
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @subsection fs_sub_getroot 🧩 Function: `getRoot(projectRoot)`
 *
 * @par 🎯 Purpose
 * Validate that the configured root actually exists and is a directory,
 * returning the validated absolute root used by every async operation.
 *
 * @par 🧒 ELI5
 * Before handing over any list, the keeper walks to the labelled door and
 * actually opens it: locked/missing → "the room isn't there" (`503
 * FILES_ROOT_NOT_FOUND`); behind it is a closet, not a room → "that's not
 * a room" (`503 FILES_ROOT_NOT_DIRECTORY`). Only then does work begin.
 *
 * @par ⚙️ How It Works (Step-by-Step)
 * 1. Call `resolveConfiguredRoot(projectRoot)` (fail-fast on missing env).
 * 2. `await fs.stat(root)` on the promise API. Any rejection — `ENOENT`,
 *    permission errors, a file where the path should be, even a dangling
 *    junction — is collapsed into one user-facing error `503
 *    FILES_ROOT_NOT_FOUND` (the raw `catch {}` deliberately discards the
 *    original cause; the operator is pointed at the `.env` path, which is
 *    the fix in essentially all cases).
 * 3. Check `stat.isDirectory()`; a regular file or symlink-to-file at
 *    `FILES_ROOT` throws `503 FILES_ROOT_NOT_DIRECTORY`.
 * 4. Return the validated root string. Note there is **no memoisation**:
 *    every API call re-stats, so an operator can fix `FILES_ROOT` without
 *    restarting the server, at the cost of one `stat` per request.
 *
 * @par 📥 Inputs
 * - `projectRoot` (from `server.js`), `process.env.FILES_ROOT`.
 * - Disk: the configured root directory (existence + type only).
 *
 * @par 📤 Outputs
 * - Promise of the validated absolute root path.
 * - Throws `503 FILES_ROOT_MISSING` / `FILES_ROOT_NOT_FOUND` /
 *   `FILES_ROOT_NOT_DIRECTORY`.
 *
 * @par 🔗 Project Dependencies
 * - Depends on: `resolveConfiguredRoot`, `fs/promises`, `serviceError`.
 * - Consumed by: the first gate of `listCategories`, `listPath`,
 *   `getDownloadTarget`, `searchEntries`, `listLatestReleases`.
 * - @see server.js — the `/api/health` route and the startup banner use the
 *   *sync* twin `peekRootSync` instead, because those call sites cannot
 *   await, and the sync variant intentionally never throws.
 *
 * @par ⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
 * The distinction between the three 503 codes is operator-facing gold:
 * collapse them into one "bad root" error and misconfigured deployments
 * become guesswork. Adding a memoised root cache would remove the
 * "fix `.env` and just retry" behaviour. Swapping `fs.stat` for
 * `fsSync.statSync` here would make a missing root *crash the request
 * path* synchronously inside async handlers — the async/throw boundary
 * must stay intact for `server.js`'s `catch` to convert these into JSON
 * errors rather than unhandled rejections.
 *
 * @par 💻 Live Source Code:
 * @snippet lib/files-service.js fs_snip_getroot
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @subsection fs_sub_splitrel 🧩 Function: `splitRel(relPath)`
 *
 * @par 🎯 Purpose
 * Normalise any client-supplied relative path into a clean array of
 * segments — the input normaliser for the path-safety wall.
 *
 * @par 🧒 ELI5
 * Reading the visitor's scribbled directions and rewriting them as a clean
 * list of room names: cross out blanks, fix back-slashes, drop the empty
 * bits, and only then start walking.
 *
 * @par ⚙️ How It Works (Step-by-Step)
 * 1. `String(relPath || "")` — `undefined`/`null` become an empty path
 *    (which callers interpret as "the root itself").
 * 2. `replace(/\\/g, "/")` — accept Windows-style separators from clients
 *    (URLs or query strings may carry backslashes) and unify to POSIX.
 * 3. `split("/")` then `.map(p => p.trim())` — tolerate accidental spaces
 *    around segments (`"my folder"` survives as two clean segments).
 * 4. `.filter(Boolean)` — drop empty segments, so trailing slashes
 *    (`/api/files/docs/`) and double slashes (`docs//sub`) never reach
 *    `path.resolve` as meaningful parts.
 *
 * @par 📥 Inputs
 * - `relPath`: raw client path (string, possibly `undefined`, either
 *   separator style, possibly with empty segments).
 *
 * @par 📤 Outputs
 * - Array of non-empty, trimmed segment strings (`[]` for the root).
 *
 * @par 🔗 Project Dependencies
 * - Depends on: nothing.
 * - Consumed by: `resolveInsideRoot` exclusively.
 * - @see assets/js/services/resource-service.js — the frontend's
 *   `encodeRelPath()` is the mirror-image producer: it also
 *   backslash-normalises, filters empties, then percent-encodes each
 *   segment before calling the API, so both ends agree on segment
 *   boundaries.
 *
 * @par ⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
 * This function defines what a "path segment" means across the whole API
 * surface. If the trim or filter steps disappear, `/api/files/docs//`
 * starts producing a `""` segment which `path.resolve` treats as
 * harmless — but `resolveInsideRoot`'s `relPosix` gains double slashes and
 * the returned `path` values stop round-tripping through the frontend's
 * `browseHref`/`downloadHref` builders. Removing the backslash swap makes
 * any Windows-flavoured client path resolve to a *single* segment named
 * `docs\sub` — a 404 that looks like missing data.
 *
 * @par 💻 Live Source Code:
 * @snippet lib/files-service.js fs_snip_splitrel
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @subsection fs_sub_resolveinside 🧩 Function: `resolveInsideRoot(root, relPath)`
 *
 * @par 🎯 Purpose
 * The path-safety wall: convert a validated segment list into an absolute
 * target path that is *provably* inside `FILES_ROOT`, or refuse loudly.
 *
 * @par 🧒 ELI5
 * The door guard who checks the request slip twice. First he reads each
 * room name for forbidden scribbles (`.` — this room, `..` — the room
 * behind us, invisible zero-characters). Then, after walking the route, he
 * checks the map again: "am I still inside the storage room?" If the
 * visitor cleverly wrote a path that ends up outside, he tears up the slip:
 * `403 PATH_TRAVERSAL`.
 *
 * @par ⚙️ How It Works (Step-by-Step)
 * 1. `splitRel(relPath)` normalises the input (above).
 * 2. Reject explicitly: any segment equal to `.` or `..`, or *containing*
 *    a NUL byte (`\0`) → `400 INVALID_PATH`. This is the readable,
 *    intention-revealing rejection — a client probing with `..` gets a
 *    precise 400 rather than a vague 403.
 * 3. `path.resolve(root, ...parts)` computes the absolute target. Because
 *    hostile segments were removed, this is expected to stay inside — but
 *    it is *not trusted*.
 * 4. Defence in depth: `path.relative(root, target)` computes the walk
 *    from the root back to the target. If the result starts with `..` (the
 *    target is outside or a sibling of the root) or is absolute (different
 *    drive letter on Windows), the target escaped → throw `403
 *    PATH_TRAVERSAL`. This catches anything step 2 could not anticipate —
 *    e.g. future Windows device-path quirks, or a root itself containing
 *    symlinked directories (note: `path.resolve` is purely lexical; symlink
 *    *content* is not followed here, only real `fs.stat` results by
 *    callers).
 * 5. Return `{ target, parts, relPosix }` — the absolute path for disk
 *    calls, the clean segments (so callers know depth and final name), and
 *    a canonical POSIX relative path for building API-facing `path` values.
 *
 * @par 📥 Inputs
 * - `root`: validated absolute root (from `getRoot`).
 * - `relPath`: raw client path from the URL or `?path=` query.
 *
 * @par 📤 Outputs
 * - `{ target, parts, relPosix }` on success.
 * - Throws `400 INVALID_PATH` or `403 PATH_TRAVERSAL`.
 *
 * @par 🔗 Project Dependencies
 * - Depends on: `splitRel`, `path`, `serviceError`.
 * - Consumed by: `listPath` and `getDownloadTarget` — every route where a
 *   client picks the path. Category-only endpoints (`listCategories`,
 *   `searchEntries`, `listLatestReleases`) never accept a client path and
 *   therefore never pass through here.
 * - Sideways: `server.js` contains a *second, independent* traversal guard
 *   (`isInsideRoot`, used by `serveStatic`) for the static-asset tree —
 *   two walls for two trees, neither relying on the other.
 * - @see server.js
 *
 * @par ⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
 * This is the module's most security-critical block. Weakening any step —
 * e.g. allowing `..` because "the frontend never sends it", or skipping the
 * `path.relative` re-check — converts `/api/download` into an arbitrary
 * file read of the server (`C:\...`, `/etc/passwd`, the `.env` itself).
 * Replacing `path.relative` with `target.startsWith(root)` reintroduces a
 * classic bypass (the sibling directory `/data/downloads-evil` starts with
 * `/data/downloads`). Even renaming the `PATH_TRAVERSAL` code is a public
 * API change. Treat every edit here as a security review, not a refactor.
 *
 * @par 💻 Live Source Code:
 * @snippet lib/files-service.js fs_snip_resolveinside
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @subsection fs_sub_toposixrel 🧩 Function: `toPosixRel(root, absPath)`
 *
 * @par 🎯 Purpose
 * Convert an absolute path that is already known to be inside the root
 * back into the canonical POSIX relative path the API uses as the `path`
 * field of every listed entry.
 *
 * @par 🧒 ELI5
 * Walking back from a real room to the storage-room door and writing down
 * the turns you took — but in the same "aisle/shelf/box" shorthand every
 * customer list is written in, no matter which operating system's dialect
 * the floor plan uses.
 *
 * @par ⚙️ How It Works (Step-by-Step)
 * 1. `path.relative(root, absPath)` — the OS-native relative walk (on
 *    Windows this may contain backslashes).
 * 2. `.split(path.sep)` — split on the *platform* separator, not a literal
 *    slash, so Windows paths split correctly.
 * 3. `.filter(Boolean)` — drop the empty segment produced when `absPath`
 *    *is* the root (so the root maps to `[]`, and `relPosix` is `""`).
 * 4. `.join("/")` — re-join with POSIX slashes: the canonical on-the-wire
 *    form that `resource-service.js` later re-encodes segment-by-segment
 *    for URLs.
 *
 * @par 📥 Inputs
 * - `root`: validated root; `absPath`: an entry's absolute path produced by
 *   `path.join(target, ent.name)`.
 *
 * @par 📤 Outputs
 * - POSIX relative path string (e.g. `docs/manuals/install-guide.pdf`).
 *
 * @par 🔗 Project Dependencies
 * - Depends on: `path`.
 * - Consumed by: `listPath` (building each entry's `path` field).
 * - Sideways: complements `splitRel`/`resolveInsideRoot` — together they
 *   guarantee the `path` values this module emits are exactly the values
 *   it accepts back on the next request (a round-trip contract the
 *   frontend depends on for `browseHref`/`downloadHref`).
 * - @see assets/js/services/resource-service.js
 *
 * @par ⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
 * The output format is a cross-file contract: `browse.js` feeds these
 * `path` strings back into `/api/files/...` and `/api/download/...`, and
 * `home.js` builds download links from release paths. If backslashes ever
 * leak into the output (e.g. replacing `split(path.sep)` with
 * `split("/")`), Windows deployments break in a confusing way: listings
 * render, but the encoded URL contains `\` and the next request 404s —
 * or worse, falls into `resolveInsideRoot`'s backslash tolerance and
 * traverses somewhere unintended.
 *
 * @par 💻 Live Source Code:
 * @snippet lib/files-service.js fs_snip_toposixrel
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @subsection fs_sub_readvisible 🧩 Function: `readVisibleEntries(dir)`
 *
 * @par 🎯 Purpose
 * One-liner wrapper that reads a directory with entry *types* included and
 * strips hidden entries — the standard "what can the visitor see here?"
 * question.
 *
 * @par 🧒 ELI5
 * A quick glance at one shelf: read every label, but skip anything on the
 * ignore-list taped to the door before showing the list to anyone.
 *
 * @par ⚙️ How It Works (Step-by-Step)
 * 1. `fs.readdir(dir, { withFileTypes: true })` returns `Dirent` objects —
 *    each carrying `isDirectory()` / `isFile()` **without** a per-entry
 *    `stat` round-trip. This is the cheap type source used everywhere.
 * 2. `.filter(e => !isHidden(e.name))` applies the portal-wide ignore list
 *    before anything downstream sees the entries.
 * 3. Errors are *not* caught here: a missing/unreadable directory
 *    propagates to callers, which either translate it (404 in
 *    `listPath`) or swallow it (walks below).
 *
 * @par 📥 Inputs
 * - `dir`: absolute directory path.
 *
 * @par 📤 Outputs
 * - Promise of a filtered `Dirent[]`.
 * - Rejects with the raw `fs` error if `dir` is unreadable.
 *
 * @par 🔗 Project Dependencies
 * - Depends on: `fs/promises`, `isHidden`.
 * - Consumed by: `assertCategoryStructure` and `listPath`.
 * - @see lib/files-service.js (walkers below intentionally duplicate the
 *   readdir+filter pattern inline so they can count/halt independently).
 *
 * @par ⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
 * Removing `withFileTypes: true` breaks every caller silently: the filter
 * callbacks would then receive plain strings, `e.isDirectory` would be
 * `undefined`, and directories would be misclassified as skipped files —
 * categories would disappear from the API with no error thrown. Adding a
 * `catch` here would mask 404s in `listPath` (empty array instead of
 * `NOT_FOUND`), breaking the frontend's error messaging.
 *
 * @par 💻 Live Source Code:
 * @snippet lib/files-service.js fs_snip_readvisible
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @subsection fs_sub_assertstruct 🧩 Function: `assertCategoryStructure(root)`
 *
 * @par 🎯 Purpose
 * Enforce the product's content contract: `FILES_ROOT` must contain at
 * least one *visible directory* (a "category"), because the portal's
 * information architecture is category-first.
 *
 * @par 🧒 ELI5
 * The librarian's rule for the basement: a storage room with no shelves
 * isn't a library — it's a locked closet (`503 FILES_ROOT_EMPTY`), and a
 * room with only loose paper on the floor isn't organised either (`503
 * FILES_ROOT_NO_CATEGORIES`): files directly in the root simply don't
 * count as shelves.
 *
 * @par ⚙️ How It Works (Step-by-Step)
 * 1. `readVisibleEntries(root)` — hidden entries (including the stray
 *    `index.html` nginx-style deployments add) do not count.
 * 2. `visible.length === 0` → throw `503 FILES_ROOT_EMPTY` with a message
 *    telling the operator the folder is not a valid resource repository.
 * 3. `visible.filter(e => e.isDirectory())` — only directories qualify as
 *    categories; loose files in the root are ignored by design (this is
 *    also why `server.js`'s README-style files at `FILES_ROOT` root never
 *    appear in the UI).
 * 4. `dirs.length === 0` → throw `503 FILES_ROOT_NO_CATEGORIES`, with a
 *    message that documents the rule itself ("Only directories are used
 *    as categories...") so the operator can self-serve.
 * 5. Return the `Dirent[]` of category folders for reuse by the two
 *    callers (avoids a second readdir).
 *
 * @par 📥 Inputs
 * - `root`: validated root directory.
 * - Disk: `FILES_ROOT` top level.
 *
 * @par 📤 Outputs
 * - Promise of visible directory `Dirent[]`.
 * - Throws `503 FILES_ROOT_EMPTY` / `FILES_ROOT_NO_CATEGORIES`.
 *
 * @par 🔗 Project Dependencies
 * - Depends on: `readVisibleEntries`, `serviceError`.
 * - Consumed by: `listCategories` (returns them as the category list),
 *   `listPath` (root case), `searchEntries` and `listLatestReleases`
 *   (validating before walking).
 * - @see server.js — these 503s are what the startup banner's
 *   `peekRootSync` state lines predict; a server that boots "green" but
 *   serves `FILES_ROOT_NO_CATEGORIES` means content was changed after
 *   start.
 *
 * @par ⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
 * This function *defines* what a category is. Allowing top-level files to
 * count as categories changes `/api/categories`, `/api/files`,
 * `/api/search` and `/api/releases` simultaneously, and the frontend's
 * category-card grid (`home.js`) would render files as folders. Loosening
 * the empty check (returning `[]` instead of throwing) turns the portal
 * into a blank page instead of a clear operator error — the thrown 503s
 * are a feature, deliberately surfaced through `sendServiceError`.
 *
 * @par 💻 Live Source Code:
 * @snippet lib/files-service.js fs_snip_assertstruct
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @subsection fs_sub_summarizetree 🧩 Function: `summarizeTree(dir)`
 *
 * @par 🎯 Purpose
 * Compute a category card's statistics — total visible files, total
 * subfolders, and the most recent modification anywhere beneath the
 * category — the numbers displayed on the home page cards.
 *
 * @par 🧒 ELI5
 * Counting one shelf properly: walk every aisle under it, tally boxes and
 * items, and jot down the newest "last touched" date you see. You skip
 * drawers that won't open (unreadable folders) and never go deeper than
 * twelve floors — you'd rather report an approximate count than never
 * come back.
 *
 * @par ⚙️ How It Works (Step-by-Step)
 * 1. Zero-initialise `fileCount`, `folderCount`, `latestModified`.
 * 2. Define the recursive `walk(current, depth)`; the very first guard is
 *    `if (depth > MAX_WALK_DEPTH) return;` — deep trees are silently
 *    truncated (documented behaviour, not an error).
 * 3. `fs.readdir(current, { withFileTypes: true })` inside try/catch:
 *    an unreadable subdirectory simply ends that branch (skip, don't fail
 *    the whole summary).
 * 4. Per entry: `isHidden` skips it; directories increment `folderCount`
 *    and recurse; *files* increment `fileCount` and get an extra
 *    `fs.stat(full)` to read `mtimeMs`, keeping the running maximum.
 *    (Directories contribute only their children's mtimes — a folder's own
 *    rename does not count as content freshness.)
 * 5. The per-file `stat` failure is swallowed (`/* skip unreadable *\/`) —
 *    a file vanishing mid-walk must not lose the whole category card.
 * 6. Return `{ fileCount, folderCount, latestModified }`, with
 *    `latestModified` normalised to `null` when nothing was stat-able (so
 *    callers can fall back).
 *
 * @par 📥 Inputs
 * - `dir`: absolute category directory path.
 * - Disk: the entire subtree, depth-capped.
 *
 * @par 📤 Outputs
 * - Promise of `{ fileCount, folderCount, latestModified }`
 *   (`latestModified` = epoch ms or `null`).
 *
 * @par 🔗 Project Dependencies
 * - Depends on: `fs/promises`, `path`, `isHidden`, `MAX_WALK_DEPTH`.
 * - Consumed by: `listCategories` (one call per category, run concurrently
 *   via the surrounding `Promise.all`).
 * - Sideways: the output feeds `mapCategory` in
 *   `resource-service.js`, whose `stats.total` (files + folders) and
 *   `stats.latestModified` (epoch ms) come straight from here and are
 *   rendered by `home.js` cards.
 * - @see assets/js/services/resource-service.js
 * - @see assets/js/home.js
 *
 * @par ⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
 * The returned *shape* is a cross-layer contract (three field names are
 * mapped by the frontend). Raising `MAX_WALK_DEPTH` here without touching
 * the search/releases walkers makes the home-page counts disagree with
 * search results on deep trees — an inconsistency users *will* report.
 * Making this stricter (throwing on unreadable branches) turns one bad
 * subfolder into a 503 for the whole home page. Its cost profile also
 * matters: one `stat` per file, no cache — a category with 50,000 files
 * means 50,000 stats per `/api/categories` call.
 *
 * @par 💻 Live Source Code:
 * @snippet lib/files-service.js fs_snip_summarizetree
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @subsection fs_sub_listcategories 🧩 Function: `listCategories(projectRoot)`
 *
 * @par 🎯 Purpose
 * Produce the full, sorted category catalogue with per-category recursive
 * statistics — the payload of `/api/categories` and the seed of the entire
 * home page.
 *
 * @par 🧒 ELI5
 * "Give me the list of shelves, each with how much is on it and when it
 * was last restocked, nicely alphabetised." The keeper checks the room,
 * walks every shelf in parallel, and staples the lists together in ABC
 * order.
 *
 * @par ⚙️ How It Works (Step-by-Step)
 * 1. `await getRoot(projectRoot)` — gate 1 (existence + type).
 * 2. `await assertCategoryStructure(root)` — gate 2 (non-empty, has
 *    directories), whose `Dirent[]` is reused.
 * 3. Map each directory `ent` concurrently (`Promise.all` over
 *    `dirs.map(...)`): `fs.stat(full)` for the folder's own `mtime`
 *    (try/catch → `mtime = null` on failure), then
 *    `await summarizeTree(full)` for the deep counts.
 * 4. Build each category object: `name` and `path` (both the raw folder
 *    name — at depth 0 the relative path *is* the name), `type:
 *    "directory"`, the summary counts, `mtime` as ISO, and
 *    `latestModified` = the tree's newest file mtime as ISO, falling back
 *    to the folder's own `mtime` when the tree had no stat-able files.
 * 5. `categories.sort((a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name))` —
 *    locale-aware, stable for the UI; the frontend renders in this order.
 * 6. Return the array (empty impossible: `assertCategoryStructure`
 *    guarantees ≥ 1).
 *
 * @par 📥 Inputs
 * - `projectRoot` from `server.js`.
 * - Disk: `FILES_ROOT` + every category subtree.
 *
 * @par 📤 Outputs
 * - Promise of `Array<{ name, path, type: "directory", fileCount,
 *   folderCount, mtime, latestModified }>`, sorted by name.
 *
 * @par 🔗 Project Dependencies
 * - Depends on: `getRoot`, `assertCategoryStructure`, `summarizeTree`,
 *   `fs/promises`, `path`.
 * - Consumed by: `server.js` route `GET /api/categories`, fetched by
 *   `resource-service.js.getResources()` (the only frontend caller),
 *   cached 5 minutes in sessionStorage, rendered by `home.js`.
 * - @see server.js
 * - @see assets/js/services/resource-service.js
 * - @see assets/js/home.js
 *
 * @par ⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
 * Every field of every object here is consumed by `mapCategory` in
 * `resource-service.js` — `cat.path` becomes both the card's `id` and its
 * `relPath` (used to build `/browse/<path>/` links), and `cat.fileCount` /
 * `cat.folderCount` / `cat.latestModified` become the card's stats. Rename
 * `latestModified` and the home page silently shows "unknown" freshness
 * with no error. The sort is also a UI contract: `localeCompare` is
 * locale-dependent, so changing to a plain `<` comparison visibly
 * reorders cards for non-ASCII category names. Finally, cost: this
 * endpoint's latency is the *sum of the largest subtree*; caching decisions
 * belong in the frontend layer (which already has one), not here.
 *
 * @par 💻 Live Source Code:
 * @snippet lib/files-service.js fs_snip_listcategories
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @subsection fs_sub_listpath 🧩 Function: `listPath(projectRoot, relPath)`
 *
 * @par 🎯 Purpose
 * List one directory anywhere in the tree — the payload of `/api/files`
 * and `/api/files/<rel>`, powering both the category list and every
 * drill-down in the browser page.
 *
 * @par 🧒 ELI5
 * "Show me what's in *this* room." At the root it answers with the shelf
 * list; inside a shelf it answers with that shelf's boxes and items, each
 * labelled with its location relative to the basement door so the visitor
 * can ask for it later by that exact label.
 *
 * @par ⚙️ How It Works (Step-by-Step)
 * 1. `await getRoot(projectRoot)` — gate 1.
 * 2. `resolveInsideRoot(root, relPath)` — gate 3 (path safety).
 * 3. **Root case** (`parts.length === 0`): reuse
 *    `assertCategoryStructure(root)` so the root listing obeys the same
 *    category rules (and the same 503s) as `/api/categories`, then map
 *    each directory with its own `fs.stat` (individual failures filtered
 *    out with `return null`), producing size-less directory rows with
 *    `path` = folder name. Note: this root case carries *no* recursive
 *    counts — the heavier stats live only in `listCategories`.
 * 4. **Non-root case**: `fs.stat(target)`; rejection → `404 NOT_FOUND`
 *    (any stat error, including permission problems, reads as "not
 *    found"); `!stat.isDirectory()` → `400 NOT_A_DIRECTORY` (asking to
 *    list a file is a client bug, not a missing resource).
 * 5. `readVisibleEntries(target)` for typed, hidden-filtered entries.
 * 6. Map each entry concurrently: `fs.stat(full)` (failure → `null`,
 *    filtered at the end — an entry deleted mid-walk silently vanishes
 *    rather than 500-ing the list); directories become `type:
 *    "directory"`, `size: 0`; regular files become `type: "file"` with
 *    `st.size` and ISO `mtime`. `toPosixRel(root, full)` builds each
 *    `path` — the canonical value the client sends back later.
 * 7. `listed.filter(Boolean)` drops skipped entries; anything neither
 *    file nor directory (sockets, FIFOs, etc.) is excluded by returning
 *    `null`.
 *
 * @par 📥 Inputs
 * - `projectRoot`, `relPath` — the latter arrives either as the
 *   `?path=` query (`/api/files?path=…`) or the URL suffix after
 *   `/api/files/` (built by `relFromPrefix` in `server.js`).
 *
 * @par 📤 Outputs
 * - Promise of `Array<{ name, path, type: "directory"|"file", size,
 *   mtime }>` — sorted implicitly by `readdir` order (alphabetical on all
 *   mainstream platforms).
 *
 * @par 🔗 Project Dependencies
 * - Depends on: `getRoot`, `resolveInsideRoot`, `assertCategoryStructure`,
 *   `readVisibleEntries`, `toPosixRel`, `fs/promises`, `path`.
 * - Consumed by: `server.js` route `GET /api/files[/...]`, fetched by
 *   `resource-service.js.listFiles(relPath)` while the user drills down in
 *   `browse.js`; each returned `path` round-trips into further `/api/files`
 *   or `/api/download` calls.
 * - @see server.js
 * - @see assets/js/services/resource-service.js
 * - @see assets/js/browse.js
 *
 * @par ⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
 * The `path` fields are *the* currency of the browse feature: `browse.js`
 * builds `ResourceService.browseHref(entry.path)` links and the download
 * button uses `downloadHref(entry.path)`; any change to how `path` is
 * built breaks every drill-down and download at once. The dual-route
 * handling (`?path=` vs URL suffix) is load-bearing in `server.js` — if
 * this function started rejecting URL-encoded-but-unsplit paths, deep
 * links from `browse.js` break. The root-case/`listCategories` split
 * matters too: this endpoint intentionally skips the expensive
 * `summarizeTree`; making it "consistent" would slow every drill-down by
 * a full-tree walk per category.
 *
 * @par 💻 Live Source Code:
 * @snippet lib/files-service.js fs_snip_listpath
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @subsection fs_sub_getdownload 🧩 Function: `getDownloadTarget(projectRoot, relPath)`
 *
 * @par 🎯 Purpose
 * Resolve a client-supplied relative path into the exact file bundle that
 * `server.js` may stream — the authorisation + metadata step of every
 * `/api/download` request.
 *
 * @par 🧒 ELI5
 * The slip gets one last check before the forklift moves: it must name a
 * real box (not a shelf), the box must actually be there, and the slip
 * must not be blank. Only then does the keeper hand the desk a tag with
 * the box's exact location, name, weight and last-touched date — the desk
 * does the carrying.
 *
 * @par ⚙️ How It Works (Step-by-Step)
 * 1. `await getRoot(projectRoot)` — gate 1.
 * 2. `resolveInsideRoot(root, relPath)` — gate 3: this is where traversal
 *    attempts (`/api/download/../.env`) die with `403 PATH_TRAVERSAL`.
 * 3. `parts.length === 0` → `400 INVALID_PATH` with message "A file path
 *    is required." — a bare `/api/download` is a client bug.
 * 4. `fs.stat(target)`; rejection → `404 NOT_FOUND` "File not found."
 * 5. `!stat.isFile()` → `400 NOT_A_FILE` — directories are *not*
 *    downloadable products; the UI never offers them, so a directory
 *    request indicates a crafted or stale link.
 * 6. Return `{ absPath: target, fileName: parts[parts.length - 1], size:
 *    stat.size, mtime: stat.mtime }`. Note the `fileName` comes from the
 *    *sanitised* segment list, not from `path.basename(target)` — same
 *    value, but provably free of separators; `server.js` puts this string
 *    straight into the `Content-Disposition` header via
 *    `contentDisposition()`, which additionally encodes non-ASCII names.
 *
 * @par 📥 Inputs
 * - `projectRoot`, `relPath` (from `?path=` or the URL suffix, decoded by
 *   `server.js`).
 *
 * @par 📤 Outputs
 * - Promise of `{ absPath, fileName, size, mtime }` (size in bytes,
 *   `mtime` as a `Date` — *not* ISO; the only raw `Date` this module
 *   exports, because `streamDownload` uses it for header building, not
 *   display).
 *
 * @par 🔗 Project Dependencies
 * - Depends on: `getRoot`, `resolveInsideRoot`, `fs/promises`.
 * - Consumed by: `server.js` route `GET/HEAD /api/download[/...]`, whose
 *   `streamDownload(req, res, fileInfo)` performs the actual
 *   `fs.createReadStream` and header work.
 * - Sideways: the frontend builds these URLs via
 *   `resource-service.js.downloadHref(relPath)` — used by `browse.js`
 *   (file rows), `home.js` (release rows) and `autocomplete.js`
 *   (suggestion rows).
 * - @see server.js
 * - @see assets/js/services/resource-service.js
 *
 * @par ⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
 * This is the gate in front of every byte the server ships. Removing the
 * `isFile` check or the empty-path check turns 400s into unexpected
 * behaviours (directory "downloads", server errors); weakening
 * `resolveInsideRoot` here is an arbitrary-file-read vulnerability. The
 * return shape is consumed field-by-field by `streamDownload`
 * (`Content-Length` = `size`, `Content-Disposition` = `fileName`,
 * `createReadStream` = `absPath`) — changing `mtime` to an ISO string
 * would still work but silently changes nothing visible except future
 * refactor confusion; renaming `absPath`, though, breaks the streamer
 * immediately with `undefined` paths and EACCES-style stream errors.
 *
 * @par 💻 Live Source Code:
 * @snippet lib/files-service.js fs_snip_getdownload
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @subsection fs_sub_namematches 🧩 Function: `nameMatches(name, query)`
 *
 * @par 🎯 Purpose
 * The single match predicate for search: case-insensitive substring test
 * with an "empty query matches everything" escape hatch.
 *
 * @par 🧒 ELI5
 * "Does the label have these letters somewhere in it?" No letters typed?
 * Then *everything* matches — the search box showing all files is a
 * feature, not a bug.
 *
 * @par ⚙️ How It Works (Step-by-Step)
 * 1. `if (!query) return true;` — an empty/undefined query matches every
 *    entry; combined with `searchEntries`'s limit this makes
 *    `/api/search?q=` a cheap "first 200 files" explorer.
 * 2. `String(name).toLowerCase().includes(query)` — the query arrives
 *    *already* lower-cased and trimmed (normalised once in
 *    `searchEntries`), so the per-entry cost is one `toLowerCase` and one
 *    `includes`. Substring semantics (not word-prefix) mean searching
 *    `fox` finds `firefox-installer.zip`.
 *
 * @par 📥 Inputs
 * - `name`: an entry name; `query`: already-lower-cased search text or
 *   empty.
 *
 * @par 📤 Outputs
 * - `boolean`.
 *
 * @par 🔗 Project Dependencies
 * - Depends on: nothing.
 * - Consumed by: `searchEntries` (both the directory-name branch and the
 *   file-name branch).
 * - Sideways: the frontend does no filtering of its own on search results
 *   (`autocomplete.js` renders `data.results` directly), so this
 *   predicate *is* the product's search semantics.
 * - @see assets/js/autocomplete.js
 *
 * @par ⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
 * Changing substring to prefix matching, or to whole-word matching, alters
 * what every user's search box finds — and `autocomplete.js`'s debounced
 * dropdown will look "broken" with no error anywhere. Matching against
 * the *un*-lower-cased name would make searches case-sensitive on Linux
 * but not Windows (their filesystems' `toLowerCase` behaviour differs),
 * creating a platform-dependent bug that is brutal to reproduce.
 *
 * @par 💻 Live Source Code:
 * @snippet lib/files-service.js fs_snip_namematches
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @subsection fs_sub_search 🧩 Function: `searchEntries(projectRoot, query, options)`
 *
 * @par 🎯 Purpose
 * Search the whole tree for entries whose name contains the query — the
 * engine behind `/api/search`, the search box and the autocomplete
 * dropdown.
 *
 * @par 🧒 ELI5
 * A treasure hunt with a walkie-talkie and a hard stop: walk the aisles
 * (deepest-first stack), check every label against the clue, put matches
 * on the trolley — but when the trolley is full (200) or you've touched
 * 8000 boxes, radio "done, might be more!" (`truncated: true`) and come
 * back.
 *
 * @par ⚙️ How It Works (Step-by-Step)
 * 1. Gates: `getRoot` then `assertCategoryStructure` — an unconfigured or
 *    empty root yields the standard 503s instead of an empty search page.
 * 2. Normalise the query *once*: `String(query || "").trim().toLowerCase()`
 *    — trimming means `" firefox "` finds `firefox.zip`; the predicate
 *    then stays cheap.
 * 3. Clamp the limit: `Math.min(Number(options.limit) || SEARCH_LIMIT,
 *    SEARCH_LIMIT)` — `server.js` passes the raw `?limit=` string (e.g.
 *    `autocomplete.js` sends 6); non-numeric falls back to 200, and no
 *    client can raise the ceiling.
 * 4. Seed an explicit **stack** with `{ dir: root, rel: "", depth: 0 }`.
 *    The while loop `stack.pop()` → **depth-first**, iterative — no
 *    recursion, so `MAX_WALK_DEPTH` is a filter (`continue`), not a
 *    stack-overflow risk, and deep trees cost time, not crashes.
 * 5. Per popped directory: depth guard, then `readdir(...,
 *    withFileTypes: true)` in try/catch (unreadable dir → `continue`,
 *    skipping only that branch). `scanned` counts directories actually
 *    read (returned for diagnostics).
 * 6. Per entry, in order: bail out of the *for* loop when
 *    `results.length >= limit || visited >= MAX_WALK_ENTRIES` (the
 *    mid-directory bail keeps the caps hard); skip hidden; `visited`
 *    counts every visible entry examined (the true work unit).
 * 7. Directories: always pushed for descent, and additionally *matched by
 *    name* — so a category named "ubuntu" is itself a result, enabling
 *    "search finds the folder then browse into it".
 * 8. Files: matched via `nameMatches` and pushed as results. Results carry
 *    `{ name, path, type }` — note **no `size`/`mtime`**: search stays
 *    cheap (no per-result stat) at the cost of less info in the dropdown.
 * 9. Loop terminates when the stack empties, `results` hits `limit`, or
 *    `visited` hits `MAX_WALK_ENTRIES`.
 * 10. Return `{ results, scanned, truncated }` where `truncated` is
 *     `visited >= MAX_WALK_ENTRIES || results.length >= limit` — the
 *     flag `home.js`-style UIs (and future UIs) can use to show "more
 *     results exist".
 *
 * @par 📥 Inputs
 * - `projectRoot`, `query` (raw `?q=` value), `options.limit` (raw
 *   `?limit=` string or number).
 *
 * @par 📤 Outputs
 * - Promise of `{ results: Array<{name, path, type}>, scanned: number,
 *   truncated: boolean }`.
 *
 * @par 🔗 Project Dependencies
 * - Depends on: `getRoot`, `assertCategoryStructure`, `nameMatches`,
 *   `isHidden`, `fs/promises`, `path`, the limit constants.
 * - Consumed by: `server.js` route `GET /api/search`, called by
 *   `resource-service.js.search(query, { limit })`, consumed by
 *   `autocomplete.js` (debounced, `limit: 6` suggestions) and `home.js`
 *   (search page).
 * - @see server.js
 * - @see assets/js/services/resource-service.js
 * - @see assets/js/autocomplete.js
 *
 * @par ⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
 * Three contract surfaces here. (1) The response *shape*
 * `{ results, scanned, truncated }` is read by the frontend verbatim —
 * flattening to a bare array breaks `autocomplete.js` on deploy. (2) DFS
 * *ordering* is implicit UX: switching to BFS (`stack.shift()`) changes
 * which 200 files a broad query returns and in what order — results users
 * have learned. (3) The caps are latency insurance: raising
 * `MAX_WALK_ENTRIES` on a huge tree makes the search *event loop* busy for
 * seconds (each `readdir` is awaited but the walk is sequential), which
 * stalls *every* route on the single-threaded server, not just search.
 * Also note `q` is currently unused for pruning directory descent —
 * every directory is explored regardless of name match; adding pruning
 * changes which files inside non-matching folders are discoverable.
 *
 * @par 💻 Live Source Code:
 * @snippet lib/files-service.js fs_snip_search
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @subsection fs_sub_releases 🧩 Function: `listLatestReleases(projectRoot, limit)`
 *
 * @par 🎯 Purpose
 * Walk the entire tree and return the most recently modified files across
 * *all* categories — the "Latest releases" feed of `/api/releases` shown
 * on the home page.
 *
 * @par 🧒 ELI5
 * "Show me the twelve newest things anywhere in the building." The keeper
 * walks *every* shelf (no shortcut), notes each item's date, sorts the
 * whole pile newest-first, and hands over just the top of the pile.
 *
 * @par ⚙️ How It Works (Step-by-Step)
 * 1. Gates: `getRoot` + `assertCategoryStructure` (same 503 contract as
 *    search).
 * 2. Clamp the cap: `Math.min(Number(limit) || RELEASES_LIMIT, 50)` —
 *    default 12 (`RELEASES_LIMIT`), hard maximum 50. `server.js` passes
 *    the raw `?limit=` string; `resource-service.js`'s `getReleases`
 *    default is 8.
 * 3. Recursive async `walk(dir, rel, depth)`: guards on
 *    `depth > MAX_WALK_DEPTH` *or* `visited >= MAX_WALK_ENTRIES` (return
 *    early — the entry budget is global, not per-branch); readdir in
 *    try/catch (unreadable branch → skip); hidden entries skipped;
 *    directories recurse with the extended POSIX `rel` prefix.
 * 4. Files: `fs.stat` in try/catch (vanishing file → skip); push
 *    `{ name, path, category, type: "file", size, mtime }`. Note the
 *    extra `category` field — `childRel.split("/")[0]` — which no other
 *    function provides; it lets the home page badge each release with its
 *    category name without a second lookup.
 * 5. After the walk: `files.sort((a, b) => new Date(b.mtime) -
 *    new Date(a.mtime))` — newest first (note `b - a`: descending).
 *    Sorting *everything* before slicing is what makes this a correct
 *    global "latest" rather than "latest per folder".
 * 6. `return files.slice(0, cap)`.
 *
 * @par 📥 Inputs
 * - `projectRoot`, `limit` (raw `?limit=` value or default).
 *
 * @par 📤 Outputs
 * - Promise of `Array<{ name, path, category, type: "file", size, mtime }>`
 *   (ISO `mtime` strings), newest first, length ≤ min(requested, 50).
 *
 * @par 🔗 Project Dependencies
 * - Depends on: `getRoot`, `assertCategoryStructure`, `isHidden`,
 *   `fs/promises`, `path`, `RELEASES_LIMIT`, `MAX_WALK_DEPTH`,
 *   `MAX_WALK_ENTRIES`.
 * - Consumed by: `server.js` route `GET /api/releases`, fetched by
 *   `resource-service.js.getReleases(limit)` and rendered by `home.js` as
 *   the "latest releases" section (each row's `path` feeds
 *   `downloadHref`).
 * - @see server.js
 * - @see assets/js/services/resource-service.js
 * - @see assets/js/home.js
 *
 * @par ⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
 * The `category` field is exclusive to this endpoint — dropping it breaks
 * the home page's category labels, not just types. Sorting happens on the
 * *full* visited set before the cap: a tree with hundreds of thousands of
 * files holds them all in memory briefly per request (this endpoint is the
 * most expensive in the module — no early exit possible, since "latest"
 * is only known after seeing everything). The 50-hard-cap means a client
 * asking `limit=1000` silently gets 50 — intentionally, but any frontend
 * change relying on larger feeds must raise the constant *here*.
 * `truncated` is *not* reported by this function (unlike `searchEntries`);
 * on trees that exceed `MAX_WALK_ENTRIES` the feed silently covers only
 * the first-visited portion — a subtle correctness boundary worth knowing
 * before touching the walk.
 *
 * @par 💻 Live Source Code:
 * @snippet lib/files-service.js fs_snip_releases
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @subsection fs_sub_peek 🧩 Function: `peekRootSync(projectRoot)`
 *
 * @par 🎯 Purpose
 * A synchronous, *never-throwing* snapshot of FILES_ROOT health for two
 * callers that cannot await: `server.js`'s startup banner and the
 * `/api/health` (and `/__health`) endpoint.
 *
 * @par 🧒 ELI5
 * A five-second hallway check with a guarantee: "I will come back with an
 * answer, even if the answer is 'I couldn't even check'." It never shouts,
 * never refuses to answer — it just reports three yes/no facts and,
 * if it failed, *why* as a code.
 *
 * @par ⚙️ How It Works (Step-by-Step)
 * 1. Wrap everything in try/catch — this is the module's only
 *    never-throw public function (health probes must never 500).
 * 2. Inside `try`: `resolveConfiguredRoot(projectRoot)` (throws
 *    `FILES_ROOT_MISSING` if unset), then `fsSync.existsSync(root)` —
 *    the *sync* API is deliberate: the startup banner runs inside
 *    `server.listen`'s callback, and the health handler wants a one-shot
 *    answer without async plumbing in its hot path.
 * 3. `isDir` via `fsSync.statSync(root).isDirectory()`, guarded by
 *    `exists &&` so a missing root never triggers a second throw.
 * 4. Return the happy shape: `{ configured: true, exists, isDirectory }`.
 * 5. In `catch (err)`: return the diagnostic shape —
 *    `configured: err.code !== "FILES_ROOT_MISSING"` (distinguishing "not
 *    configured" from "configured but broken"), `exists: false`,
 *    `isDirectory: false`, and `error: err.code || "UNKNOWN"` — the
 *    serviceError code, which is precisely why the codes are stable
 *    identifiers.
 *
 * @par 📥 Inputs
 * - `projectRoot`; environment `FILES_ROOT`; disk stat of the root (sync).
 *
 * @par 📤 Outputs
 * - `{ configured, exists, isDirectory }` or
 *   `{ configured, exists: false, isDirectory: false, error }` — plain
 *   data, never throws.
 *
 * @par 🔗 Project Dependencies
 * - Depends on: `resolveConfiguredRoot`, `fsSync` (callback `fs` module),
 *   and indirectly on `serviceError`'s codes.
 * - Consumed by: `server.js` twice — inside the `/api/health` handler
 *   (the `status: "ok" | "degraded"`, `filesRootConfigured`,
 *   `filesRootReady` fields are computed directly from this result) and in
 *   the `server.listen` startup banner (the green/yellow `FILES_ROOT is…`
 *   line).
 * - @see server.js
 *
 * @par ⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
 * The result shape is read field-by-field by `server.js`'s health JSON —
 * uptime monitors and dashboards key on `status: "ok"` which is computed
 * as `configured && exists && isDirectory` from these three booleans.
 * Changing field names flips production health checks to "degraded" with
 * zero code errors. Removing the catch would violate the never-throw
 * contract: `/api/health` would fall into `sendServiceError` and start
 * 500-ing exactly when operators need it most. And using the *promise*
 * `fs` here would change the return type to a Promise, silently making
 * every boolean field `undefined` (truthiness checks in the banner would
 * still "work", producing confidently wrong health reports).
 *
 * @par 💻 Live Source Code:
 * @snippet lib/files-service.js fs_snip_peek
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @subsection fs_sub_exports 🧩 Block: `module.exports`
 *
 * @par 🎯 Purpose
 * The module's public surface: the seven functions `server.js` is allowed
 * to know about — the complete, frozen boundary between transport and
 * domain layers.
 *
 * @par 🧒 ELI5
 * The basement door's service window. Exactly seven hatches are open; the
 * librarian can shout through these and nothing else. Everything else in
 * the room — helpers, constants, private tools — stays behind the wall.
 *
 * @par ⚙️ How It Works (Step-by-Step)
 * 1. Export list: `listCategories` (→ `/api/categories`), `listPath`
 *    (→ `/api/files`), `getDownloadTarget` (→ `/api/download`),
 *    `searchEntries` (→ `/api/search`), `listLatestReleases`
 *    (→ `/api/releases`), `peekRootSync` (→ `/api/health` + banner),
 *    `getRoot` (exported for completeness/testing; no route uses it).
 * 2. Deliberately *not* exported: `isHidden`, `serviceError`,
 *    `resolveConfiguredRoot`, `splitRel`, `resolveInsideRoot`,
 *    `toPosixRel`, `readVisibleEntries`, `assertCategoryStructure`,
 *    `summarizeTree`, `nameMatches` — all internal. This privacy is what
 *    makes the path-safety wall auditable: no caller can bypass
 *    `resolveInsideRoot` because no caller can reach anything that skips
 *    it.
 * 3. `server.js` consumes this via
 *    `const filesService = require("./lib/files-service")` immediately
 *    after `loadEnv(PROJECT_ROOT)` — ordering that guarantees
 *    `process.env.FILES_ROOT` exists before the first call.
 *
 * @par 📥 Inputs
 * - None.
 *
 * @par 📤 Outputs
 * - The module's `exports` object, consumed by `server.js`.
 *
 * @par 🔗 Project Dependencies
 * - Depends on: every function above.
 * - Consumed by: `server.js` exclusively (verified: no other file in the
 *   repository requires this module).
 * - @see server.js
 *
 * @par ⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
 * This object *is* the API between layers. Removing or renaming a key is a
 * crash-at-boot (`filesService.listCategories is not a function` on the
 * first matching request). Adding exports is safe but widens the
 * trust boundary — anything exported must uphold the same validation
 * guarantees. Note `getRoot` is exported though no route calls it: useful
 * for tooling/tests, but any *new* route consuming it directly must
 * remember it validates the root but not the category structure.
 *
 * @par 💻 Live Source Code:
 * @snippet lib/files-service.js fs_snip_exports
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @section fs_lifecycle 🔄 End-to-End Lifecycle: One Click, Three Files
 *
 * The full journey of a single download click, showing this module's exact
 * slice of responsibility:
 *
 * @dot
 * digraph download_lifecycle {
 *   rankdir=LR;
 *   node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, color="#9CA3AF"];
 *   edge [fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=9, color="#6B7280"];
 *
 *   click   [label="browse.js\\nuser clicks Download", fillcolor="#DBEAFE"];
 *   href    [label="resource-service.js\\ndownloadHref(entry.path)", fillcolor="#DBEAFE"];
 *   route   [label="server.js\\nGET /api/download/<rel>", fillcolor="#FEF3C7"];
 *   gate    [label="files-service.js\\ngetRoot + resolveInsideRoot\\nstat + isFile check", fillcolor="#FEF3C7"];
 *   bundle  [label="{absPath, fileName,\\nsize, mtime}", shape=note, fillcolor="#CFFAFE"];
 *   stream  [label="server.js\\nstreamDownload\\ncreateReadStream → pipe", fillcolor="#D1FAE5"];
 *   disk    [label="FILES_ROOT file", shape=cylinder, fillcolor="#FFEDD5"];
 *   user    [label="Browser saves file", fillcolor="#D1FAE5"];
 *
 *   click -> href -> route -> gate -> bundle -> stream -> user;
 *   disk -> gate [label="stat", style=dashed];
 *   disk -> stream [label="bytes", style=dashed];
 * }
 * @enddot
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @section fs_contracts 📐 Conventions & Contracts
 *
 * | Symbol / Attribute | Type | Defined Here | Consumed By | Purpose |
 * |--------------------|------|--------------|-------------|---------|
 * | `MAX_WALK_ENTRIES` | `const 8000` | Yes | `searchEntries`, `listLatestReleases` | Hard cap on visited entries per walk |
 * | `MAX_WALK_DEPTH` | `const 12` | Yes | `summarizeTree`, `searchEntries`, `listLatestReleases` | Recursion/descent cap |
 * | `SEARCH_LIMIT` | `const 200` | Yes | `searchEntries` | Default + max search results |
 * | `RELEASES_LIMIT` | `const 12` | Yes | `listLatestReleases` | Default releases cap (hard max 50) |
 * | `isHidden(name)` | `(string) => boolean` | Yes | `readVisibleEntries`, walkers, search | Portal-wide hidden-entry rule |
 * | `serviceError(httpStatus, code, message)` | `(n, s, s) => Error` | Yes | All throwing paths; `server.js` `sendServiceError` | Error contract: status + stable code |
 * | `resolveConfiguredRoot(projectRoot)` | `(s) => string` | Yes | `getRoot`, `peekRootSync` | FILES_ROOT → absolute path (throws 503 if unset) |
 * | `getRoot(projectRoot)` | `(s) => Promise<string>` | Yes, exported | All async public functions | Validate root exists + is directory |
 * | `splitRel(relPath)` | `(s) => string[]` | Yes | `resolveInsideRoot` | Normalise client path into segments |
 * | `resolveInsideRoot(root, relPath)` | `(s, s) => {target, parts, relPosix}` | Yes | `listPath`, `getDownloadTarget` | Path-safety wall (400/403) |
 * | `toPosixRel(root, absPath)` | `(s, s) => string` | Yes | `listPath` | Absolute → canonical POSIX relative |
 * | `readVisibleEntries(dir)` | `(s) => Promise<Dirent[]>` | Yes | `assertCategoryStructure`, `listPath` | Typed readdir + hidden filter |
 * | `assertCategoryStructure(root)` | `(s) => Promise<Dirent[]>` | Yes | `listCategories`, `listPath`, `searchEntries`, `listLatestReleases` | Enforce category-first contract (503s) |
 * | `summarizeTree(dir)` | `(s) => Promise<{fileCount, folderCount, latestModified}>` | Yes | `listCategories` | Per-category recursive stats |
 * | `listCategories(projectRoot)` | `(s) => Promise<Category[]>` | Yes, exported | `server.js` `/api/categories` | Sorted catalogue with stats |
 * | `listPath(projectRoot, relPath)` | `(s, s) => Promise<Entry[]>` | Yes, exported | `server.js` `/api/files` | Flat directory listing |
 * | `getDownloadTarget(projectRoot, relPath)` | `(s, s) => Promise<{absPath, fileName, size, mtime}>` | Yes, exported | `server.js` `/api/download` | Authorise + resolve file for streaming |
 * | `nameMatches(name, query)` | `(s, s) => boolean` | Yes | `searchEntries` | Case-insensitive substring predicate |
 * | `searchEntries(projectRoot, query, options)` | `(s, s, o) => Promise<{results, scanned, truncated}>` | Yes, exported | `server.js` `/api/search` | Capped DFS name search |
 * | `listLatestReleases(projectRoot, limit)` | `(s, n) => Promise<Release[]>` | Yes, exported | `server.js` `/api/releases` | Newest-files feed, sorted desc |
 * | `peekRootSync(projectRoot)` | `(s) => {configured, exists, isDirectory, error?}` | Yes, exported | `server.js` `/api/health` + banner | Never-throw sync root probe |
 * | `FILES_ROOT` (env) | `string` | No (`lib/load-env.js` reads `.env`) | `resolveConfiguredRoot` | Deployment-defined downloads root |
 * | Error codes `FILES_ROOT_MISSING` / `_NOT_FOUND` / `_NOT_DIRECTORY` / `_EMPTY` / `_NO_CATEGORIES` / `INVALID_PATH` / `PATH_TRAVERSAL` / `NOT_FOUND` / `NOT_A_DIRECTORY` / `NOT_A_FILE` | `string` | Yes | `server.js` JSON `code` field | Stable machine-readable failure kinds |
 *
